.TH SASH-GETOPT 1 "2026-11-12" "safebash" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sash-getopt \- normalize command options into NUL-terminated fields
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sash-getopt
.RI [ option " ...]"
.BI "\-o " shortopts
.RB [ "\-l " longopts ]
.B \-\-
.RI [ argument " ...]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sash-getopt
is a standalone option parser derived from util-linux
.BR getopt (1).
It recognizes short and long options and writes a normalized argument
sequence.
Unlike ordinary getopt output, every field is unquoted and terminated
by a NUL byte; there is no final newline.
No shell-quoting parser or shell evaluation is needed.
.PP
Use
.B \-o
to specify the short options, including an empty string when there are none.
Use
.B \-\-
to separate this helper's options from the arguments to parse.
The older form with a positional short-option specification is also accepted.
.SH OPTION SPECIFICATIONS
Each letter in
.I shortopts
declares a short option.
A following colon requires an argument; two colons make it optional.
An optional short-option argument must be attached, as in
.BR \-cblue .
.PP
.I longopts
is a comma- or whitespace-separated list of long-option names.
One or two trailing colons likewise declare required or optional arguments.
Supply optional long-option arguments with
.BR \-\-color=blue ,
not as a separate word.
Recognized abbreviations are expanded to the full declared name.
.PP
Normally options are moved before positional arguments, preserving the
order within each group.
A leading
.B +
in
.I shortopts
stops option parsing at the first non-option.
A leading
.B \-
instead emits non-options as they are encountered.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "\-o, \-\-options " shortopts
Declare the short options to recognize.
.TP
.BI "\-l, \-\-longoptions " longopts
Declare long options.
This option may be repeated to add more names.
.TP
.B \-a, \-\-alternative
Also recognize long options introduced by a single dash.
.TP
.BI "\-n, \-\-name " name
Use
.I name
in diagnostics about the arguments being parsed.
.TP
.B \-q, \-\-quiet
Suppress option-parser diagnostics for those arguments.
This does not suppress errors in the helper's own invocation.
.TP
.B \-Q, \-\-quiet-output
Suppress normal field output.
The exit status still reports parsing errors.
.TP
.B \-U, \-\-unknown
Retain unknown-option material in the output outside
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
mode.
Also suppress option-parser diagnostics.
This does not make an invalid option successful: parsing still exits 1.
.TP
.BI "\-s, \-\-shell " shell
Accepted for compatibility and ignored.
Output is always NUL-terminated and unquoted.
.TP
.B \-u, \-\-unquoted
Accepted for compatibility and ignored.
.TP
.B \-T, \-\-test
Produce no normal output and exit 4.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print usage to standard output and exit 0.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Print version information to standard output and exit 0.
.SH OUTPUT
Short-option clusters become separate fields.
Each option argument is a separate field, including an empty field for
an absent optional argument.
A
.B \-\-
field precedes the remaining positional arguments.
Spaces, quotes and newlines inside an argument remain part of that field.
.PP
Check the exit status before accepting the normalized sequence.
A failing parse can already have written fields; output is not transactional.
NUL bytes cannot be retained in a Bash scalar command substitution.
Use a NUL-aware reader rather than splitting on whitespace or evaluating
the output as shell code.
.PP
In safebash,
.B read
is the NUL-delimited array reader.
Reading these fields from a pipe still introduces taint.
Recognizing an option does not validate its argument for the application's
use; normal attestation or explicit trust rules remain.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
When present, stops parsing at the first non-option.
.TP
.B GETOPT_COMPATIBLE
When present, selects the legacy interface: the first argument is the
short-option specification rather than a helper option.
This changes even the interpretation of
.B \-\-help
and
.BR \-T .
Unset it when using the interface described in the synopsis.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Arguments parsed successfully, or help/version requested.
.TP
.B 1
An error in the arguments being parsed.
.TP
.B 2
An invalid invocation of the helper.
.TP
.B 3
Memory allocation failed.
.TP
.B 4
The
.B \-\-test
probe.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
sash-getopt \-o ab: \-\- \-a \-b val 'has space'
.EE
.PP
The output bytes are shown below with each NUL represented as
.BR \e0 :
.PP
.EX
\-a\e0\-b\e0val\e0\-\-\e0has space\e0
.EE
.PP
Long options, including an optional value:
.PP
.EX
sash-getopt \-o '' \-l help,output:,color:: \-\- \e
    \-\-color=blue \-\-output report.txt input.txt
.EE
.SH BUILDING
.B mk build
builds this helper.
It is a standalone C program, not a Cargo workspace member.
To build it directly from the repository root:
.PP
.EX
mkdir \-p target/release
cc \-O2 \-o target/release/sash-getopt sash-getopt/sash-getopt.c
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sash (1),
.BR sash-expand (1),
.BR sash-convert (1),
.BR getopt (3)
